# {{ company_prog_name }} — Automation (operator cron)

The operator can tick unattended: `bin/cs_operator_cron.sh` runs
`claude -p "/cs-operator"` on a schedule — triage + campaign advancement,
draft-only, with its own re-denied permission set. This command manages
that schedule from the session. All commands run from the repo root with
`.venv/bin/python -m cs …`.

## 1. Where things stand

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cs cron status
```

Also check the kill-switch: does `~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/CS_PAUSE`
exist? Report both in one line: installed or not, schedule, paused or
live.

## 2. What the user wants

- **"turn it on" / install** — show first what will happen: the manifest
  schedule (`{{ cron_schedule }}`), what one tick does (triage inbound →
  drafts; advance campaigns → drafts; escalate on uncertainty; NOTHING
  sent while `CS_TRIAGE_MODE=draft`), and where it logs
  (`~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/cs_operator.log`). Then, on the user's
  explicit yes:

  ```bash
  .venv/bin/python -m cs cron install
  ```

  Verify: `cs cron status` again and read the installed line back.
  Requirement worth checking now: the Claude CLI must be reachable by
  cron (default `~/.local/bin/claude`, override `CLAUDE_BIN` — the
  wrapper says which it uses).

- **"pause"** — `touch ~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/CS_PAUSE` (every tick
  becomes a deliberate no-op; nothing is uninstalled). **"resume"** —
  remove that file.

- **"turn it off" / uninstall** — on explicit yes:

  ```bash
  .venv/bin/python -m cs cron uninstall
  ```

- **"did it run? what did it do?"** — read the tail of
  `~/.{{ company_slug }}-cs/cs_operator.log` and summarize the last tick
  (when, what it drafted, what it escalated).

## 3. Boundaries

- Install/uninstall/pause only on the user's explicit instruction in
  this conversation — never as a side effect of another request.
- This command never edits `bin/cs_operator_cron.sh`, the permission
  files, or `CS_TRIAGE_MODE`: going autonomous-send is a separate,
  deliberate widening the user performs on purpose, not a cron setting.
- A dedicated per-campaign cron is the exception, not the rule — the
  operator tick already advances every campaign. See `/cs-campaign`.
